// Formative practice quiz for choosing-the-right-collection. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/collections-in-depth/choosing-the-right-collection

::choosing-the-right-collection o1 - unique visitors::An app must record each visitor ID once and answer has this ID visited quickly. Order does not matter. Which implementation fits?{
=HashSet#Correct. Uniqueness plus fast contains with no order requirement is exactly a HashSet.
~ArrayList#Not quite. A list keeps duplicates, and its contains scans every element.
~TreeMap#Not quite. There are no values to map, and sorting was not required.
~ArrayDeque#Not quite. A deque is for working at the ends, not for membership tests.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o1 - a task queue::Print jobs must be processed in the order they arrive, adding at one end and removing at the other. Which implementation fits?{
=ArrayDeque, used with offer and poll#Correct. First-in-first-out at the ends is a queue, and ArrayDeque is the general-purpose choice.
~TreeSet#Not quite. A TreeSet sorts jobs instead of keeping arrival order, and drops duplicates.
~HashMap#Not quite. There is no key to look up.
~PriorityQueue#Not quite. That serves the smallest element first, not the earliest arrival.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o1 - lookups by key::A service looks up a customer record by customer ID many times per second. Output order is never needed. Which fits best?{
=HashMap from ID to record#Correct. Key lookup with no order requirement\: constant time with well-spread hash codes.
~ArrayList of records searched with a loop#Not quite. A scan per lookup is O(n).
~TreeMap from ID to record#Not quite. It works, but lookups cost log(n) and sorting was not required.
~LinkedHashMap from ID to record#Not quite. It works, but insertion order was not required; plain HashMap is enough.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o2 - reading by index::Why is ArrayList the better choice than LinkedList for code that calls get(i) at random positions?{
=ArrayList.get runs in constant time; LinkedList walks node by node to reach the index#Correct. The ArrayList documentation states constant-time get; LinkedList traverses from the nearer end.
~LinkedList does not have a get method#Not quite. It does, but it has to walk to the index.
~Both are O(1); ArrayList just uses less memory#Not quite. LinkedList.get(i) is O(n) because of the walk.
~ArrayList sorts its elements, which speeds up get#Not quite. ArrayList keeps insertion order; it does not sort.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o2 - iterating::Both lists visit every element once in a for-each loop. Why does Dev.java still find ArrayList faster for iteration?{
=LinkedList follows a reference to a separate node at every step, so it suffers from pointer chasing#Correct. Same O(n), but array elements sit together in memory while nodes are scattered.
~ArrayList skips elements during iteration#Not quite. Every element is visited in both.
~LinkedList iterates in reverse#Not quite. It iterates from first to last.
~ArrayList iteration is O(1)#Not quite. Visiting n elements is O(n) for any list.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o3 - first-appearance order::A survey stores each answer with its count and must print answers in the order they first appeared. Which map fits?{
=LinkedHashMap#Correct. It keeps insertion order, and a repeated put does not move a key.
~HashMap#Not quite. It makes no order guarantee.
~TreeMap#Not quite. It sorts by key instead of keeping first-appearance order.
~Any map, because maps print in insertion order#Not quite. Only LinkedHashMap and other sequenced maps keep insertion order.
}

::choosing-the-right-collection o3 - sorted output::A list of player names must be printed sorted, and duplicates must be kept. Which approach fits?{
=Keep the ArrayList and sort it with list.sort before printing#Correct. Sorting the list keeps duplicates and gives sorted order.
~Copy the names into a TreeSet#Not quite. A TreeSet sorts but drops duplicate names.
~Copy the names into a HashSet#Not quite. No order, and duplicates are dropped.
~Add them to a PriorityQueue and print the queue#Not quite. Printing a PriorityQueue follows no particular order; only repeated poll gives sorted order.
}
